-Your mother?

252
00:13:49,787 --> 00:13:50,996
My late husband's.

253
00:13:52,456 --> 00:13:53,666
Come with me.

254
00:13:59,547 --> 00:14:00,840
(MUSIC CONTINUES)

255
00:14:04,301 --> 00:14:08,055
(PANTING)
I've put you in Ian's old room.

256
00:14:12,893 --> 00:14:14,311
(SOMBER MUSIC PLAYING)

257
00:14:14,395 --> 00:14:16,772
It's a bit further
away than the others,

258
00:14:17,022 --> 00:14:20,276
but I thought you might
like that, the privacy.

259
00:14:21,277 --> 00:14:23,028
Oh, and it has an en suite.

260
00:14:23,988 --> 00:14:26,407
I should get you
some fresh towels.

261
00:14:26,907 --> 00:14:28,576
It's the help's week off.

262
00:14:31,287 --> 00:14:33,706
Most of his things
have been put away in boxes now.

263
00:14:35,207 --> 00:14:38,085
Rugby trophies... (CHUCKLES)
...those awful band posters,

264
00:14:38,252 --> 00:14:39,503
video games.

265
00:14:39,962 --> 00:14:43,132
Other things that I just
couldn't bear to put away.

266
00:14:47,761 --> 00:14:48,846
(SIGHS)

267
00:14:53,100 --> 00:14:54,810
No matter
what he told you, Stella.

268
00:14:56,812 --> 00:14:58,022
It wasn't all bad.

269
00:14:59,982 --> 00:15:01,400
It's happy memories.

270
00:15:01,901 --> 00:15:02,985
Good times.

271
00:15:03,736 --> 00:15:05,237
Families are complicated.

272
00:15:08,490 --> 00:15:09,658
Dinner's at 7:00.

273
00:15:09,992 --> 00:15:11,410
(FOOTSTEPS RECEDING)

274
00:15:25,299 --> 00:15:26,634
(GENTLE MUSIC PLAYING)

275
00:15:27,343 --> 00:15:29,011
-FELIX: How's that drink, Granno?
-(CUTLERY CLINKING)

276
00:15:29,094 --> 00:15:30,638
-GRANNO: Mm. It's nice.
-(LAKE CHUCKLES)

277
00:15:30,721 --> 00:15:32,431
GRANNO: I don't usually
like things with lemon,

278
00:15:32,514 --> 00:15:34,183
-but this is okay.
-(LAKE CHUCKLES)

279
00:15:36,393 --> 00:15:38,646
CAROLINE:
Has anybody heard from him?

280
00:15:38,729 --> 00:15:41,106
I mean we've got time,
but we've just gotta make sure

281
00:15:41,190 --> 00:15:43,108
that Stella
doesn't get suspicious.

282
00:15:43,192 --> 00:15:44,902
LAKE: I hope
things stay on schedule.

283
00:15:44,985 --> 00:15:47,446
-I really can't be away from the firm forever.
-(EERIE MUSIC PLAYING)

284
00:15:47,529 --> 00:15:50,074
You work too much.
It's affecting your complexion.

285
00:15:50,157 --> 00:15:51,575
Thanks, Granno.

286
00:15:51,742 --> 00:15:53,577
Felix takes enough time off
for the both of us,

287
00:15:53,661 --> 00:15:55,829
-don't you, darling?
-Did you want bitters in your tonic,

288
00:15:55,913 --> 00:15:57,790
or do you just produce
them naturally?

289
00:15:57,873 --> 00:15:59,458
-(KISSES)
-Felix!

290
00:16:00,292 --> 00:16:04,546
Cadell babies come early,
and she does look close.

291
00:16:04,630 --> 00:16:05,798
LAKE: I wouldn't worry.

292
00:16:05,881 --> 00:16:08,258
Ian always did go
for the Pollyannas.

293
00:16:08,342 --> 00:16:11,762
-Ho, ho, ho, ho, ho. Merry Christmas!
-(QUIRKY MUSIC PLAYING)

294
00:16:11,845 --> 00:16:13,555
(SIGHS) It's summer, darling.

295
00:16:13,639 --> 00:16:15,933
Well, Santa spent
the Lord's b-day

296
00:16:16,016 --> 00:16:19,395
with a raging hangover,
but he's here now.

297
00:16:19,478 --> 00:16:23,357
-(PLASTIC BAG CRUMPLES)
-Sister of mine, present for thee.

298
00:16:23,440 --> 00:16:24,775
(OBJECTS CLATTERING)

299
00:16:25,776 --> 00:16:27,569
-(GRANNO LAUGHS)
-(GASPS)

300
00:16:27,653 --> 00:16:29,321
Is this new or did you
try it out for me?

301
00:16:29,405 --> 00:16:31,865
-Oh, aren't you hysterical? Mwah, mwah.
-(BLOWS KISSES)

302
00:16:32,366 --> 00:16:34,201
BUNNY: And what
have we got for Felix?

303
00:16:34,326 --> 00:16:36,036
It's an old bottle of scotch.

304
00:16:36,120 --> 00:16:38,956
Half empty, but you're used
to living off Lake's scraps, eh?

305
00:16:39,039 --> 00:16:40,124
(LAUGHS)

306
00:16:40,457 --> 00:16:43,293
GRANNO: Oh, it's so good
to have our Benjamin Bunny back.

307
00:16:43,377 --> 00:16:44,920
-BUNNY: No, stop.
-(GRANNO CHUCKLES)

308
00:16:45,004 --> 00:16:47,131
BUNNY: And last but not least.

309
00:16:47,214 --> 00:16:49,466
Oh, what to get
for the delicate flower

310
00:16:49,550 --> 00:16:51,760
who ejected me from her loins?

311
00:16:52,428 --> 00:16:54,555
-Hmm.
-Oh, thank you.

312
00:16:54,638 --> 00:16:56,974
Finally, something to beat
some common sense

313
00:16:57,057 --> 00:16:58,517
into the lot of you.

314
00:16:58,600 --> 00:16:59,935
BUNNY: So,
where's the baby mama?

315
00:17:00,811 --> 00:17:02,604
-I... I'm here.
-(SOMBER MUSIC PLAYING)

316
00:17:03,188 --> 00:17:07,192
You? You're my brother's
pregnant widow.

317
00:17:08,360 --> 00:17:11,613
Huh. I'm afraid
Santa forgot your gift.

318
00:17:11,697 --> 00:17:12,740
It's okay.

319
00:17:13,615 --> 00:17:14,658
Hang on.

320
00:17:18,871 --> 00:17:21,665
BUNNY: Ian gave it to me
when I passed my A levels.

321
00:17:22,499 --> 00:17:23,792
Take it, I insist.

322
00:17:28,630 --> 00:17:29,798
Thank you.

323
00:17:30,299 --> 00:17:32,301
Well, who wants sea bass?

324
00:17:32,384 --> 00:17:33,927
-Oh, me. Me, me, please.
-LAKE: Yes.

325
00:17:34,011 --> 00:17:36,263
-FELIX: I am starving.
-(LIVELY MUSIC PLAYING)

326
00:17:36,346 --> 00:17:40,893
-FELIX: 20K. 20K, and in three weeks, four weeks.
-(INDISTINCT CHATTER, LAUGHTER)

327
00:17:48,984 --> 00:17:53,113
(INDISTINCT CHATTER CONTINUES)

328
00:17:56,366 --> 00:17:57,659
(MUSIC FADES)

329
00:17:58,702 --> 00:18:00,746
So, where are you from, Stella?

330
00:18:00,871 --> 00:18:02,873
I grew up in Ilford, in Essex.

331
00:18:02,956 --> 00:18:03,832
Oh, dear.

332
00:18:03,916 --> 00:18:06,293
More chocolate silk pie, dear?

333
00:18:06,376 --> 00:18:09,129
No, thank you.
I couldn't eat another bite, um.

334
00:18:09,797 --> 00:18:12,174
I should
probably call it a night.

335
00:18:12,257 --> 00:18:14,009
Uh, it's been
a really long day and,

336
00:18:14,510 --> 00:18:17,137
well, I shot
a fifth birthday party

337
00:18:17,221 --> 00:18:19,932
at a Gymboree,
just to make some extra cash.

338
00:18:20,599 --> 00:18:24,645
-I have about 100 photos to edit.
-What the fuck's a Gymboree?

339
00:18:24,728 --> 00:18:26,480
I've got a confession
to make, Stella.

340
00:18:27,356 --> 00:18:31,110
-I saw you earlier listening out in the hallway.
-(PENSIVE MUSIC PLAYING)

341
00:18:31,193 --> 00:18:34,905
So, I know that you know
that we're hiding something.

342
00:18:36,698 --> 00:18:38,158
-I wasn't--
-CAROLINE: Relax.

343
00:18:40,160 --> 00:18:43,122
It's not as if a baby shower
is the secret of the century.

344
00:18:43,205 --> 00:18:45,332
-(GASPS)
-(CAROLINE LAUGHS)

345
00:18:45,415 --> 00:18:47,126
(SIGHS, CHUCKLES)

346
00:18:47,209 --> 00:18:49,169
Oh, you...

347
00:18:49,253 --> 00:18:50,963
Wow, you don't...
you don't have to do that.

348
00:18:51,046 --> 00:18:53,715
Oh, no, we must celebrate you
and the little one.

349
00:18:53,799 --> 00:18:55,551
It's just going to be
a family affair.

350
00:18:55,634 --> 00:18:57,052
Very quiet. Low-key.

351
00:18:58,011 --> 00:18:59,304
(CHUCKLES)

352
00:18:59,930 --> 00:19:02,349
-(BIRDS CHIRPING)
-(INTRICATE MUSIC PLAYING)

353
00:19:03,767 --> 00:19:04,977
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

354
00:19:09,064 --> 00:19:10,190
(CAMERA SHUTTER CLICKING)

355
00:19:10,607 --> 00:19:12,067
Champagne is meant
to served immediately!

356
00:19:12,151 --> 00:19:13,902
This is fucking warm! Go fix it.

357
00:19:13,986 --> 00:19:15,737
MRS. DEREK:
I was just telling Derek,

358
00:19:15,821 --> 00:19:17,906
it's great having an excuse
for us all to get together.

359
00:19:18,031 --> 00:19:20,200
-Are you planning on eating the placenta?
-(CAMERA SHUTTER CLICKING)

360
00:19:20,284 --> 00:19:22,953
You look lovely.
Absolutely glowing.

361
00:19:23,036 --> 00:19:26,165
DEREK: Don't be afraid of labor.
That's the easy part.

362
00:19:26,248 --> 00:19:28,167
It's raising them that's hard.

363
00:19:28,250 --> 00:19:31,378
-Think happy thoughts.
-(CAMERA SHUTTER CLICKING)

364
00:19:31,461 --> 00:19:33,839
-(OVERLAPPING CHATTER)
-(MUSIC INTENSIFIES)

365
00:19:38,343 --> 00:19:39,553
(CAMERA CLATTERING)

366
00:19:39,636 --> 00:19:41,388
(INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC PLAYING
OVER SPEAKER)

367
00:19:43,307 --> 00:19:45,517
-Do you mind pouring me one of those?
-(DRINK POURING)

368
00:19:46,310 --> 00:19:47,978
Sure. (CHUCKLES)

369
00:19:48,187 --> 00:19:49,897
-They threw me one last month.
-(DRINK POURING)

370
00:19:50,564 --> 00:19:52,024
Felt like someone ran me over,

371
00:19:52,107 --> 00:19:54,234
and then dropped presents
on top of my body. (CHUCKLES)

372
00:19:55,110 --> 00:19:57,070
-Yeah.
-(GLASSES CLINK)

373
00:19:57,154 --> 00:19:59,406
I'm Rose. Henry's wife.

374
00:20:00,032 --> 00:20:03,744
He and Ian were cousins.
Twice removed or something.

375
00:20:05,204 --> 00:20:06,788
My condolences, by the way.

376
00:20:07,080 --> 00:20:09,166
I can't imagine going
through this alone.

377
00:20:10,125 --> 00:20:13,879
-Listen, when they tell you anything about--
-Rose, come here.

378
00:20:15,255 --> 00:20:19,218
I'm being summoned.
Um, maybe we will talk again?

379
00:20:20,344 --> 00:20:22,638
Oh, and don't worry
about the insect.

380
00:20:23,138 --> 00:20:25,349
-It doesn't bite or anything.
-HENRY: Rose.

381
00:20:26,892 --> 00:20:27,935
ROSE: Coming, darling.

382
00:20:29,102 --> 00:20:31,271
Insect? What?

383
00:20:35,442 --> 00:20:37,486
-(GENTLE MUSIC PLAYING)
-(BIRDS CHIRPING)

384
00:20:37,569 --> 00:20:39,029
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

385
00:20:55,504 --> 00:20:57,714
No offense, but...

386
00:20:58,590 --> 00:21:01,009
you're going to be too heavy
for me to carry inside

387
00:21:01,093 --> 00:21:02,594
if you do decide
to go into labor.

388
00:21:02,678 --> 00:21:05,138
Uh, this one's got me running
about a million degrees,

389
00:21:05,222 --> 00:21:07,224
so the cold water feels nice.

390
00:21:07,474 --> 00:21:09,768
So I'm not the only one
that needed a breather.

391
00:21:10,644 --> 00:21:11,812
(WATER RIPPLING)

392
00:21:13,814 --> 00:21:15,857
I can't believe
how many people came.

393
00:21:16,900 --> 00:21:19,027
-They don't even know me.
-Our family's a little weird,

394
00:21:19,111 --> 00:21:21,154
but you might have
sussed that already.

395
00:21:22,322 --> 00:21:23,699
But they mean well.

396
00:21:26,410 --> 00:21:27,369
Come on.

397
00:21:28,328 --> 00:21:29,955
-No, no, no, no, no, no, no, no.
-STELLA: Please?

398
00:21:30,038 --> 00:21:31,164
No. Okay.

399
00:21:32,499 --> 00:21:34,334
Let me get my glasses.
One second.

400
00:21:35,919 --> 00:21:37,504
(CAMERA SHUTTER CLICKING)

401
00:21:37,587 --> 00:21:38,880
STELLA: Nice. One more?

402
00:21:39,840 --> 00:21:41,258
-(CAMERA SHUTTER CLICKING)
-STELLA: Yes.

403
00:21:41,341 --> 00:21:42,718
I think this is
my better side anyway.

404
00:21:42,801 --> 00:21:43,969
(CHUCKLES)

405
00:21:44,386 --> 00:21:46,638
You want a pound's worth
of free advice?

406
00:21:46,972 --> 00:21:48,682
Thought the going rate
was a penny.

407
00:21:49,308 --> 00:21:50,851
Yeah, well, mine's worth more.

408
00:21:53,770 --> 00:21:55,397
Whatever's going to happen...

409
00:21:57,024 --> 00:21:58,025
let it.

410
00:21:58,692 --> 00:22:00,610
Fighting just makes it worse.

411
00:22:03,155 --> 00:22:04,489
Oh, case in point.

412
00:22:05,824 --> 00:22:06,950
It's time.

413
00:22:07,743 --> 00:22:08,827
Come on in.

414
00:22:09,870 --> 00:22:12,998
-This tradition has been in the family for generations.
-(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

415
00:22:13,081 --> 00:22:15,959
Granno's done it, I've done it,
and now, it's your turn.

416
00:22:16,543 --> 00:22:18,837
Just now, lie down here.

417
00:22:19,921 --> 00:22:21,006
-STELLA: Really?
-CAROLINE: Mm.

418
00:22:21,089 --> 00:22:22,799
(GUESTS SHUSHING)

419
00:22:22,883 --> 00:22:26,094
It's nothing to be scared of.
It's just a little insect

420
00:22:26,178 --> 00:22:27,804
that we're going to drop
on your belly,

421
00:22:27,888 --> 00:22:30,349
and it'll land
on its back or its feet.

422
00:22:30,432 --> 00:22:32,976
So, one way means a boy,
the other means a girl.

423
00:22:33,310 --> 00:22:35,729
-Which is which?
-GRANNO: Hello!

424
00:22:35,854 --> 00:22:38,273
-(GUESTS APPLAUD)
-(PENSIVE MUSIC PLAYING)

425
00:22:45,030 --> 00:22:49,951
Are you ready?

426
00:22:51,495 --> 00:22:53,789
(ALL EXCLAIM)

427
00:22:56,792 --> 00:22:57,959
(INDISTINCT WHISPERING)

428
00:22:58,043 --> 00:23:00,420
(BREATHES SHAKILY)

429
00:23:03,882 --> 00:23:05,092
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

430
00:23:05,175 --> 00:23:06,843
(SOFT MUSIC PLAYING
OVER SPEAKERS)

431
00:23:09,346 --> 00:23:11,556
Oh, I just wish
I could convince you

432
00:23:11,640 --> 00:23:12,933
to stay with us longer.

433
00:23:13,016 --> 00:23:16,353
I have to get back tomorrow.
I'm so behind with work.

434
00:23:16,937 --> 00:23:18,021
Of course.

435
00:23:18,438 --> 00:23:20,023
But I'll visit
when the baby comes.

436
00:23:20,941 --> 00:23:22,067
How about a nightcap?

437
00:23:22,692 --> 00:23:25,946
A virgin one
for the mama-to-be, naturally.

438
00:23:26,696 --> 00:23:27,739
(CHUCKLES)

439
00:23:27,906 --> 00:23:32,202
I hope you felt showered
by Cadell love today.

440
00:23:35,288 --> 00:23:37,249
-Granno.
-Thank you.

441
00:23:40,502 --> 00:23:43,296
CAROLINE: May this baby
be everything that we hope,

442
00:23:43,380 --> 00:23:45,757
nothing more,
nothing less. Cheers.

443
00:23:45,841 --> 00:23:46,716
-Cheers.
-Cheers.

444
00:23:46,800 --> 00:23:48,009
-GUESTS: Cheers.
-Cheers.

445
00:23:49,302 --> 00:23:50,762
(SLURPS SOFTLY, GULPS)

446
00:23:56,351 --> 00:23:58,019
-Thank you, darling.
-My pleasure.

447
00:23:59,229 --> 00:24:00,772
You've all been so generous.

448
00:24:00,856 --> 00:24:04,025
I could never have afforded
most of those things on my own,

449
00:24:04,109 --> 00:24:06,111
so thank you.

450
00:24:06,695 --> 00:24:08,822
Well, if that's your way
of asking if we're going to fund

451
00:24:08,905 --> 00:24:11,158
the baby going
forward, then... (LAUGHS)

452
00:24:11,241 --> 00:24:14,411
-No, I would never ex--
-Oh darling, I'm just teasing.

453
00:24:15,120 --> 00:24:18,248
Though you probably should.
And we are happy to help.

454
00:24:18,790 --> 00:24:21,042
It's not always easy marrying
into the Cadell family,

455
00:24:21,126 --> 00:24:24,588
but trust me,
we take care of our own.

456
00:24:26,673 --> 00:24:27,841
That's very kind.

457
00:24:30,385 --> 00:24:31,678
Okay.

458
00:24:34,848 --> 00:24:36,558
(EERIE MUSIC PLAYING)

459
00:24:36,641 --> 00:24:38,560
(ECHOING) I think I stood up
too quickly. (CHUCKLES)

460
00:24:38,643 --> 00:24:41,646
Uh, thank you
again for everything.

461
00:24:46,693 --> 00:24:47,777
Good night, everyone.

462
00:24:48,278 --> 00:24:49,905
(MUSIC DISTORTS)

463
00:24:50,530 --> 00:24:51,656
(ECHOING) Good luck.

464
00:24:52,574 --> 00:24:53,533
(ECHOING) You too.

465
00:24:54,534 --> 00:24:55,785
(BREATHES SHAKILY)

466
00:24:59,206 --> 00:25:02,417
-(GROANS, BREATHES SHAKILY)
-Okay, okay, okay, okay, okay.

467
00:25:04,252 --> 00:25:06,421
Hey. It's okay.
I need some help here!

468
00:25:07,380 --> 00:25:09,007
You have Ian's nose.

469
00:25:12,886 --> 00:25:14,304
DEREK: Pre-eclampsia.

470
00:25:14,763 --> 00:25:16,848
Your pressure's off the charts.

471
00:25:16,932 --> 00:25:18,975
Couple that
with the loss of balance.

472
00:25:19,059 --> 00:25:21,144
What does that mean?
Is the baby--

473
00:25:21,228 --> 00:25:22,938
DEREK: Oh, completely fine.

474
00:25:23,772 --> 00:25:26,566
(SIGHS) I... I should probably
go to a hospital.

475
00:25:26,650 --> 00:25:29,402
Well, all they'd do
is run the same tests I did.

476
00:25:29,986 --> 00:25:32,656
I mean, the best prescription
for pre-eclampsia

477
00:25:32,739 --> 00:25:36,201
is complete rest.

478
00:25:36,493 --> 00:25:39,412
Luckily, you only have
a short time till you deliver.

479
00:25:39,496 --> 00:25:42,582
Derek is the top OB-GYN
in the country.

480
00:25:43,792 --> 00:25:46,002
DEREK: You can get up
to go to the bathroom,

481
00:25:46,086 --> 00:25:49,256
uh, shower once a day,
walk a little.

482
00:25:49,798 --> 00:25:52,384
Apart from that,
you take it easy.

483
00:25:52,759 --> 00:25:56,555
No cooking, no cleaning,
no strenuous exercise.

484
00:25:56,846 --> 00:25:58,557
Well, I... I live alone.

485
00:25:58,723 --> 00:26:01,393
Well, the last thing
we want to see is for you

486
00:26:01,476 --> 00:26:04,604
to have a similar episode
on a long drive.

487
00:26:05,480 --> 00:26:07,065
It could be fatal.

488
00:26:07,440 --> 00:26:09,359
We could take care of her here.

489
00:26:09,442 --> 00:26:11,528
I... I can't ask you to do that.

490
00:26:11,611 --> 00:26:14,406
Oh, Stella,
I know that you wanna be home

491
00:26:14,489 --> 00:26:15,532
where things are familiar,

